USR-1 - Process failures are lost in successful cycle outcomes
Severity: Major. Category: MustChange/rewrite. Checklist: sections 3, 5.
Locations: OrquestadorProcesos.cs:47 (../../CMS/Usuarios/Aplicacion/Orquestador/OrquestadorProcesos.cs:47), OrquestadorProcesos.cs:58 (../../CMS/Usuarios/Aplicacion/Orquestador/OrquestadorProcesos.cs:58), OperacionServicio.cs:128 (../../CMS/Usuarios/Aplicacion/Services/OperacionServicio.cs:128), and SolicitudesConfirmadasProceso.cs:64 (../../CMS/Usuarios/Aplicacion/Orquestador/ProcesosGenerales/SolicitudesConfirmadasProceso.cs:64).
Code block (csharp)
await proceso.EjecutarAsync(); // Boolean discarded; same for initial processes.
// Orchestrator subsequently returns true.
_ = await _operaciones.EjecutaAsync(_estado.EstadoInicializacion());
// Service subsequently returns true.
General processes have real false-return failure paths, for example an exception caught in ActualizaClientesProceso. The orchestrator ignores those outcomes. Confirmed-request processing also logs a false subprocess result but returns true after the loop. Consequently a failed operation can appear as an entirely successful cycle even when its local error log exists.
Fix: define success/failure/skipped semantics, preserve the aggregate outcome, and propagate it through OperacionServicio. If independent processes must continue, use an explicit aggregate; if a downstream process requires earlier success, stop it. The business dependency policy must be confirmed rather than invented.
Code block (csharp)
bool succeeded = true;
foreach (var proceso in orderedProcesses)
{
    bool processSucceeded = await proceso.EjecutarAsync();
    succeeded = processSucceeded && succeeded;
}
return succeeded;
The snippet illustrates continue-and-report only. First distinguish a configured-disabled step: LongevidadUsuarioProceso currently returns false for that case too. Benefit: truthful cycle status and reliable recovery decisions. Regression: failed general/initial/subprocess cases propagate failure; disabled optional steps are explicitly skipped; required order is preserved.
Suggested JIRA: USR-1, High.
