USR-3 - SQL preprocessing splits and alters quoted values
Severity: Major. Category: MustChange/rewrite. Checklist: sections 3, 8.
Location: ProcesarScriptsProceso.cs:146 (../../CMS/Usuarios/Aplicacion/Orquestador/ProcesosGenerales/ProcesarScriptsProceso.cs:146).
Code block (csharp)
var sql1 = Regex.Replace(sql, @"/\*.*?\*/", "", RegexOptions.Singleline);
var sql2 = Regex.Replace(sql1, @"--.*?$", "", RegexOptions.Multiline);
// A GO-removal regex follows.
var statements = sql3.Split(new[] { ';' }, StringSplitOptions.RemoveEmptyEntries);
Valid SQL such as UPDATE dbo.Example SET Note = 'a;b' WHERE Id = 1; is split inside the quoted value. Comment markers inside quoted values are also removed as if they were SQL comments. This is a definite parser-correctness issue, not a claim of an exploitable injection boundary.
Fix: replace regex/string splitting with compatible, approved T-SQL tokenization/parsing. Validate complete batches and allowed statement types before execution; preserve string literals, escaped quotes, identifiers and real comments. Do not silently broaden the allowed SQL language.
Recommended implementation shape (pseudocode; parser selection is a separate dependency decision):
Code block (text)
parse the original batch without rewriting literal content
reject parsing errors or unsupported statement nodes before executing anything
execute only the validated, original statement spans
Benefit: SQL values remain intact and preprocessing does not create malformed partial work. Regression: semicolons/doubled quotes/comment markers inside literals, actual comments, multiline batches and rejection of unsupported syntax without executing an accepted prefix.
Suggested JIRA: USR-3, High.
