USR-9 - Failed persistence results do not stop success or acknowledgement
Severity: Major. Category: MustChange/rewrite. Checklist: sections 3, 5, 8.
Locations: ActualizaClientesProceso.cs:56 (../../CMS/Usuarios/Aplicacion/Orquestador/ProcesosGenerales/ActualizaClientesProceso.cs:56), ReemplazoTokensProceso.cs (../../CMS/Usuarios/Aplicacion/Orquestador/ProcesosGenerales/ReemplazoTokensProceso.cs), PlantillaService.cs (../../CMS/Usuarios/Aplicacion/Services/Plantilla/PlantillaService.cs), and the user subprocess implementations (../../CMS/Usuarios/Aplicacion/Orquestador/ProcesosGenerales/SolicitudesConfirmadasSubProcesos/SubProcesos).
Current: repository Task<bool> results are awaited individually or via Task.WhenAll and discarded. Client/account replacement, email/token mutation, user/permission mutation, longevity updates and eight template task groups can continue without testing a reported failure. The concrete wrappers log and return Exito; shared nonquery failures can produce false rather than throw. Some explicit procedure errors do throw; not every failure is swallowed.
Code block (text)
await repository.MutateAsync(...)   // returned false is ignored
continue dependent work / report success
Concrete backup example, PlantillaService.cs:94 (../../CMS/Usuarios/Aplicacion/Services/Plantilla/PlantillaService.cs:94):
Code block (csharp)
await Task.WhenAll(
    _hist.InsertRespaldoPlantillasAsync(cliente),
    _tomi.InsertHistoricoPlantillasAsync(cliente)
);
Fix: consume each outcome at the application boundary; on failed backup/mutation, do not perform dependent destructive work or acknowledge completion. Preserve the failed operation and safe correlation for reconciliation. Use existing result contracts or a clear typed outcome, not a success default. Coordinate with USR-1 and query failure handling in USR-13.
Recommended flow (pseudocode, not a new API):
Code block (text)
outcomes = await required backups/mutations
if any failed: report failure; stop dependent work; retain item for recovery
only after verified success: perform next dependent step / acknowledge
Benefit / regression: a false result fails the appropriate item/cycle and remains retriable. Inject false and thrown failures at each store and template backup stage; verify no terminal acknowledgement, no later dependent mutation, and no duplicate effect on retry. Suggested JIRA: USR-9, High.
USR-10 - Delete, insert and source acknowledgement race each other
Severity: Blocker. Category: MustChange/rewrite. Checklist: sections 3, 8.
Locations: ActualizaClientesProceso.cs:56 (../../CMS/Usuarios/Aplicacion/Orquestador/ProcesosGenerales/ActualizaClientesProceso.cs:56) and ActualizaCuentasProceso.cs:57 (../../CMS/Usuarios/Aplicacion/Orquestador/ProcesosGenerales/ActualizaCuentasProceso.cs:57).
Current: both processes start delete, replacement insert and TOMSER processed-state update in the same Task.WhenAll (the client path includes both TOMI and TOMSER updates). Concrete repositories execute separate procedures on separate connections. Contextual client/account DELETE definitions commit independently. Task.WhenAll neither orders these calls nor makes them one transaction. A late delete can remove the inserted row, and source acknowledgement can precede a failed replacement.
Code block (csharp)
await Task.WhenAll(
    _cmsCliente.DeleteAsync(cliente.SATM02COD),
    _cmsCliente.InsertAsync(cliente),
    _tomiCliente.UpdateAsync(cliente.SATM02COD),
    _tomserCliente.UpdateAsync(cliente.SATM02COD)
);
Fix: replace atomically within the destination using an approved transaction/upsert contract, verify success, then acknowledge the source. Define idempotent replay and reconciliation for acknowledgement failure; do not assume a distributed transaction is available.
Code block (text)
await atomic destination replacement
require verified success
await source acknowledgement; on failure retain durable reconciliation state
Benefit / regression: no missing destination after acknowledged replacement. Use controllable task completion gates to force delete-last, insert failure and acknowledgement failure; integration tests verify rollback/uniqueness on a disposable database. Suggested JIRA: USR-10, Blocker.
USR-11 - A partially accepted SQL request can be marked fully applied
Severity: Major. Category: MustChange/rewrite. Checklist: sections 3, 8.
Location: ProcesarScriptsProceso.cs:65 (../../CMS/Usuarios/Aplicacion/Orquestador/ProcesosGenerales/ProcesarScriptsProceso.cs:65).
Current: validation separates accepted and NoExecuted statements. The accepted subset is executed; any nonempty execution-result list causes the parent request to be marked applied before rejected statements are handled. A mixed-validity batch can therefore become completed although not all requested statements ran. This is established independently of unverified per-row SQL error semantics.
Code block (csharp)
var result = await _scriptEjecuta.EjecutaScriptsPendientesAsync(sqlExecute.DtExecutedOk);
if (result.Count>0)
{
    // A log call precedes this acknowledgement.
    await _scriptObtiene.UpdateAplicadoAsync(item.Id_Script);
}
Fix: reject the complete request before mutation if any statement is invalid, unless an explicitly approved partial-result state/contract is introduced. Mark fully applied only when every required statement is accepted and confirmed successful. Align with USR-3 and USR-15.
Code block (text)
parse/validate whole request
if any rejected statement: report rejected request; execute nothing
otherwise execute approved batch; verify all outcomes; only then mark fully applied
Benefit / regression: rejected work remains visible and recoverable. Mixed accepted/rejected batches must not execute or acquire a fully-applied status; empty/failed execution results must not acknowledge success. Suggested JIRA: USR-11, High.
