5. Architecture-Fit Note
The evidenced workload fits a background worker; code-quality findings alone do not justify turning it into microservices or replacing Windows Service hosting. Apply the standing architecture question in the control document after validating the runtime dependencies.
Option	Fit to known behavior	Trade-offs / evidence needed
Modernize in place	Existing timer-driven administrative synchronization	Lowest behavioral disruption; first fix outcomes, sender/parser defects and build reproducibility
Recoverable job/queue processing	Potentially useful if requests require durable retries and independent recovery	Need idempotency, ordering, transaction/reconciliation contracts and operational ownership
Scheduled host/function	Possible if every cycle is bounded and deployment policy allows it	Need duration/volume, Windows/DLL dependencies, scheduling precision and cold-start constraints
Full microservices rewrite	No supporting evidence from the current review	Separate business case, migration/consistency plan, platform budget and SLAs required

Unknowns: SLAs, batch volumes, external-procedure duration, outage tolerance, deployment constraints, budget and recovery objectives. Sequence: fix real defects -> validate recovery/add regression tests -> reduce measured duplication -> evaluate hosting modernization. No rewrite mandate.
